/vps-*.png

# Local SQLite drafts (runtime data — production uses a Docker volume)
backend/data/

# Fonts. Zetta Serif, the proposal typeface, is LICENSED and this repo is PUBLIC. The server
# supplies it at runtime (see backend/fonts/README.md), so no font file of any kind belongs in
# git; backend/tests/test_proposal_fonts.py checks that. Name no commit here, or anywhere in the
# tree, that still holds one: that test checks it too.
*.otf
*.otc
*.ttf
*.ttc
*.woff
*.woff2

# Kyle's trailing-12 workbook: real win rates and revenue by trade. This repo is public.
# A sanitized copy of its LAYOUT lives at backend/tests/fixtures/trailing12_layout.xlsx.
Trailing 12TH MONTH.xlsx

# ── Customer material and working files that live in the repo root ───────────
# THIS REPO IS PUBLIC. Real customer documents get dropped in the working
# directory while a bid is being built — a project info sheet, a deposit
# invoice, an install form, a lock-up drawing. None of it belongs in git, and
# none of it was ignored until now: on 2026-08-19 a `git add -A` here staged
# sixteen of them, and the `git reset --hard` that followed deleted the lot.
# They were recovered from the orphaned commit, but the only durable fix is for
# git to stop seeing them at all. Ignoring them also keeps `git status` legible,
# which is what makes an accidental `add -A` less tempting in the first place.
#
# Scoped to the repo ROOT (leading /) so a genuine fixture committed under
# backend/tests/fixtures/ is unaffected.
/*.docx
/*.xlsx
/*.pdf
/*.png
/*.jpg
/*.eml
/*.msg
# Drafted outbound messages, written here for review before they are sent.
/email-*.txt

# Local tool output that is never committed: the auto-generated knowledge snapshot
# (~/scripts/refresh-treadwell-knowledge.ps1), Codex's local state, the graphify graph,
# browser-walk captures, console dumps, and the crash dumps Git Bash leaves behind.
/.claude/knowledge/
/.codex/
/graphify-out/
/walk2/
/console-debug.txt
*.stackdump

# deploy/zap-scan.sh's own output: the full HTML/JSON OWASP ZAP report, with every weak URL,
# parameter and attack string it found on a real, named site. CI never produces this (see
# .github/workflows/zap-baseline.yml); it only ever exists on whichever machine ran the script,
# and this repo is public, so it never gets committed either.
/zap-reports/

# ── Node, and the real workbooks the Excel-parity audit reads ────────────────
# node_modules/ is what `npm install hyperformula@2.7.1` leaves behind when the audit in
# docs/excel-parity-audit/ is run. Nothing in the repo depends on it (there is no package.json),
# so there is nothing to commit beside it. The audit's INPUTS are real customer estimate
# workbooks copied out of Dropbox as job01.xlsx, job02.xlsx and so on, and what it dumps from
# them (jobNN.json, jobNN.excel.json) carries every cell of a real bid. This repo is public, so
# none of that is committed. The audit's scripts, its README and last-report.json are not
# touched by these patterns.
node_modules/
docs/excel-parity-audit/job*
*.excel.json
# A plain `npm install hyperformula@2.7.1` run inside docs/excel-parity-audit/ also writes these two.
# The repository has no package.json on purpose (nothing here depends on a node package), so a file
# that appears there is an install that went to the wrong folder. The README gives the form that
# leaves nothing behind: `npm i --no-save --prefix <scratch dir> hyperformula@2.7.1`.
docs/excel-parity-audit/package.json
docs/excel-parity-audit/package-lock.json
